@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.1968 = private unnamed_addr constant [1124 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of `dict.items()` that discard either the key or the value\0Awhen iterating over the dictionary.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AIf you only need the keys or values of a dictionary, you should use\0A`dict.keys()` or `dict.values()` respectively, instead of `dict.items()`.\0AThese specialized methods are more efficient than `dict.items()`, as they\0Aavoid allocating tuples for every item in the dictionary. They also\0Acommunicate the intent of the code more clearly.\0A\0ANote that, as with all `perflint` rules, this is only intended as a\0Amicro-optimization, and will have a negligible impact on performance in\0Amost cases.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aobj = {\22a\22: 1, \22b\22: 2}\0Afor key, value in obj.items():\0A    print(value)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aobj = {\22a\22: 1, \22b\22: 2}\0Afor value in obj.values():\0A    print(value)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThe fix does not perform any type analysis and, as such, may suggest an\0Aincorrect fix if the object in question does not duck-type as a mapping\0A(e.g., if it is missing a `.keys()` or `.values()` method, or if those\0Amethods behave differently than they do on standard mapping types).\0A", align 1
