@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2161 = private unnamed_addr constant [2573 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for use of `TypeAlias` annotations and `TypeAliasType` assignments\0Afor declaring type aliases.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `type` keyword was introduced in Python 3.12 by [PEP 695] for defining\0Atype aliases. The `type` keyword is easier to read and provides cleaner\0Asupport for generics.\0A\0A## Known problems\0A[PEP 695] uses inferred variance for type parameters, instead of the\0A`covariant` and `contravariant` keywords used by `TypeVar` variables. As\0Asuch, rewriting a type alias using a PEP-695 `type` statement may change\0Athe variance of the alias's type parameters.\0A\0AUnlike type aliases that use simple assignments, definitions created using\0A[PEP 695] `type` statements cannot be used as drop-in replacements at\0Aruntime for the value on the right-hand side of the statement. This means\0Athat while for some simple old-style type aliases you can use them as the\0Asecond argument to an `isinstance()` call (for example), doing the same\0Awith a [PEP 695] `type` statement will always raise `TypeError` at\0Aruntime.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0AListOfInt: TypeAlias = list[int]\0APositiveInt = TypeAliasType(\22PositiveInt\22, Annotated[int, Gt(0)])\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Atype ListOfInt = list[int]\0Atype PositiveInt = Annotated[int, Gt(0)]\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix is marked unsafe for `TypeAlias` assignments outside of stub files because of the\0Aruntime behavior around `isinstance()` calls noted above. The fix is also unsafe for\0A`TypeAliasType` assignments if there are any comments in the replacement range that would be\0Adeleted.\0A\0A## See also\0A\0AThis rule only applies to `TypeAlias`es and `TypeAliasType`s. See\0A[`non-pep695-generic-class`][UP046] and [`non-pep695-generic-function`][UP047] for similar\0Atransformations for generic classes and functions.\0A\0AThis rule replaces standalone type variables in aliases but doesn't remove the corresponding\0Atype variables even if they are unused after the fix. See [`unused-private-type-var`][PYI018]\0Afor a rule to clean up unused private type variables.\0A\0AThis rule will not rename private type variables to remove leading underscores, even though the\0Anew type parameters are restricted in scope to their associated aliases. See\0A[`private-type-parameter`][UP049] for a rule to update these names.\0A\0A[PEP 695]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0695/\0A[PYI018]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/unused-private-type-var/\0A[UP046]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/non-pep695-generic-class/\0A[UP047]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/non-pep695-generic-function/\0A[UP049]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/rules/private-type-parameter/\0A", align 1
