@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2214 = private unnamed_addr constant [30 x i8] c"parenthesize-chained-operators", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2215 = private unnamed_addr constant [588 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for chained operators where adding parentheses could improve the\0Aclarity of the code.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A`and` always binds more tightly than `or` when chaining the two together,\0Abut this can be hard to remember (and sometimes surprising).\0AAdding parentheses in these situations can greatly improve code readability,\0Awith no change to semantics or performance.\0A\0AFor example:\0A```python\0Aa, b, c = 1, 0, 2\0Ax = a or b and c\0A\0Ad, e, f = 0, 1, 2\0Ay = d and e or f\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aa, b, c = 1, 0, 2\0Ax = a or (b and c)\0A\0Ad, e, f = 0, 1, 2\0Ay = (d and e) or f\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2216 = private unnamed_addr constant [102 x i8] c"Parenthesize `a and b` expressions when chaining `and` and `or` together, to make the precedence clear", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2217 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2216, [8 x i8] c"f\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2218 = private unnamed_addr constant [24 x i8] c"quadratic-list-summation", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2219 = private unnamed_addr constant [1797 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for the use of `sum()` to flatten lists of lists, which has\0Aquadratic complexity.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe use of `sum()` to flatten lists of lists is quadratic in the number of\0Alists, as `sum()` creates a new list for each element in the summation.\0A\0AInstead, consider using another method of flattening lists to avoid\0Aquadratic complexity. The following methods are all linear in the number of\0Alists:\0A\0A- `functools.reduce(operator.iadd, lists, [])`\0A- `list(itertools.chain.from_iterable(lists))`\0A- `[item for sublist in lists for item in sublist]`\0A\0AWhen fixing relevant violations, Ruff defaults to the `functools.reduce`\0Aform, which outperforms the other methods in [microbenchmarks].\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Alists = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]\0Ajoined = sum(lists, [])\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport functools\0Aimport operator\0A\0A\0Alists = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]\0Afunctools.reduce(operator.iadd, lists, [])\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis fix is always marked as unsafe because `sum` uses the `__add__` magic method while\0A`operator.iadd` uses the `__iadd__` magic method, and these behave differently on lists.\0AThe former requires the right summand to be a list, whereas the latter allows for any iterable.\0ATherefore, the fix could inadvertently cause code that previously raised an error to silently\0Asucceed. Moreover, the fix could remove comments from the original code.\0A\0A## References\0A- [_How Not to Flatten a List of Lists in Python_](https://mathieularose.com/how-not-to-flatten-a-list-of-lists-in-python)\0A- [_How do I make a flat list out of a list of lists?_](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/952914/how-do-i-make-a-flat-list-out-of-a-list-of-lists/953097#953097)\0A\0A[microbenchmarks]: https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues/5073#issuecomment-1591836349\0A", align 1
@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2220 = private unnamed_addr constant [30 x i8] c"Avoid quadratic list summation", align 1
